---
const points = [
  {
    verb: 'The brand',
    body: 'The name and the visual language were <strong>shaped through an agentic process</strong>: options generated and explored by machine, <strong>the final call made by a person</strong>. A family of related identities, not a logo handed down.',
  },
  {
    verb: 'The build',
    body: 'The tools, and this very site, are developed the <strong>agent-centric way we advocate</strong>: work driven from <strong>written specs and agent sessions</strong>, each change tracing back to an intent you can read. <strong>Assistance, never autopilot.</strong>',
  },
  {
    verb: 'The design system',
    body: 'One shared, <strong>token-based design system</strong> underpins every surface. Agents maintain and migrate it across products, and an <strong>automated audit fails the build on any drift</strong>. This site\'s content is <strong>schema-validated</strong> too, so a wrong edit does not ship; it stops the build instead.',
  },
];
---

<section id="method" class="section method" data-sl-fx-backdrop>
  <div class="method__bed" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="wrap method__grid">
    <div class="method__head">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="section-index__no">03</span>
        <span class="eyebrow">The method</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="h2">Made the way it's meant to run.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Sondalab builds tools for directing agents, and it is{' '}
        <strong>built by directing them</strong>. People set the intent and{' '}
        <strong>hold the switch</strong>; the machine does the work, in the open,
        every step <strong>traceable and reversible</strong>. The brand, the
        design system, and this very site came out of that same loop.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="method__points sl-fx-goo sl-fx-goo--drift">
      <svg id="glass-numeral-masks" class="mnum-masks" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><defs></defs></svg>
      {points.map((p, i) => (
        <li class="mslot sl-fx-goo__member">
          <span class="sr-only">Step {i + 1}</span>
          <article class="mpoint glass-thick sl-fx-glass sl-fx-glass--pane sl-fx-aurora" data-curtains data-sl-fx-live="on">
            <div class="mpoint__body">
              <h3 class="mpoint__verb">{p.verb}</h3>
              <p set:html={p.body}></p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <span class="mpoint__glow" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <span class="mpoint__no glass-thick sl-fx-glass sl-fx-glass--pane"

                aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The bed, and the same plate-not-gradients rule as the stance and the tools:
     effects-gpu.js samples --slfx-backdrop as a url() and nothing else, so a
     bed painted in CSS alone is invisible to the shader. Here it matters twice
     over, because the substance above it is translucent and this is what shows
     through. scripts/make-backdrops.mjs generates it from the kit's tokens. */
  .method {
    position: relative;
    --slfx-backdrop: url('/backdrops/method-dark.svg');
  }
  :root[data-sl-theme='light'] .method {
    --slfx-backdrop: url('/backdrops/method-light.svg');
  }
  .method__bed {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: var(--slfx-backdrop) center / cover no-repeat;
  }
  .method .wrap { position: relative; z-index: 1; }

  .method__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .method__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 1.1fr;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .method__head {
      position: sticky;
      top: 6rem;
    }
  }
  .method__head .h2 { margin-top: 1.25rem; max-width: 16ch; }
  .method__head .lede { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 42ch; }

  /* The three points are one substance that will not hold still. The gap is
     laid out a hair INSIDE the field's own weld, so the members start joined
     and the drift is what parts them: at 28px a 1.6rem gap welds, and an
     amplitude of 6px per member swings the distance between two of them by up
     to 12px either way — enough to cross the threshold and come back.

     The padding is what the container owes them. The substance reaches
     --slfx-goo-reach plus three blur sigmas past the outermost member, and the
     drift adds its amplitude on top; the negative inline margin gives it back,
     so the type stays aligned with the column beside it. */
  .method__points {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    --slfx-goo-weld: 28px;
    /* The kit's defaults were too quiet here, measured rather than guessed.
       --sl-bg-surface-raised against the canvas is 1.15:1 — the default suits a
       member that carries a colour of its own, and these carry none, so the
       whole slab was that grey. Lifting it toward the ink puts it near 1.7:1,
       which reads as material without becoming a card.

       6px over a 17s period is a peak speed of about 2px a second, which is
       below what anyone watches. At 12px the necks cross the 28px weld visibly
       — and it is still inside the kit's own bound of half the weld. */
    --slfx-goo-fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sl-text-primary) 15%, var(--sl-bg-surface-raised));
    --slfx-goo-drift: 12px;
    /* SPEND THE WELD ON BLUR, NOT ON DILATION. weldPlan() splits the 28px
       between a rim and a sigma, capped by this: at the kit's 5px cap it puts
       10.63px into the rim, and a 10px rim around a card that now has a strong
       glass edge of its own reads as a stray second outline — which is what
       the owner saw as an artefact. At 15 the same 28px becomes 3.88px of rim
       and 15px of sigma: the necks get softer, the halo stops being a line.
       Measured with geom.js, not guessed. */
    --slfx-goo-soft: 15px;
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin-inline: calc(-1 * clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem));
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.6rem;
  }

  /* The substance lets the bed through. `opacity` on the FIELD, not a
     translucent fill: goo-field.js forces --slfx-goo-fill opaque on purpose,
     because the goo chain's alpha slope erases everything below 0.5 and a
     translucent silhouette would simply not survive the filter. Opacity is
     applied to the filter's RESULT, so the weld is already computed when it
     lands and every neck stays exactly where it was.

     Safe here because no member of this field carries a fill of its own — the
     cards are glass and paint on pseudo-elements, and the numerals' blobs are
     the one exception, which is drawn as a tint on purpose. On a field of
     `sl-tag`s this would shift every colour the component chose, and I would
     not do it there. :global() because the field is the kit's element, not
     this component's. */
  .method__points :global(.sl-fx-goo__field) { opacity: 0.72; }

  /* No rule between them. A border draws a line straight across a merged mass,
     which is the defect the substance exists to replace. */
  /* One column now: the numeral is no longer a cell of this grid. The left
     padding opens up to leave it the corner it hangs in. */
  .mpoint {
    display: grid;
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);
    /* The numeral's gutter. Wide enough that a two-digit figure at
       --step-numeral clears the body text: about 152px at the 7rem cap. */
    padding-left: clamp(6rem, 16vw, 12rem);
    /* The card is a pane as well as a member: the pane is what puts light
       inside it — a spot that follows the pointer and an edge that turns to
       face it — where the goo only ever says these belong together. The two
       do not fight: the kit stands the pane's hairline down on a welded
       member, because where a silhouette holds the edge a rigid ring around
       it reads as broken. */
    --slg-radius: var(--sl-radius-lg);
  }

  /* THE GLYPHS ARE THE GLASS. Not a pane with a number on it — the number
     itself, cut out of the material.

     A mask clips a backdrop-filter's output, so masking the pane to the
     letterforms leaves glass in the shape of `01`. The mask is an SVG <text>
     living in the document, not a data URI: a data URI has no access to the
     page's webfonts and would fall back to a system mono, which is a different
     shape from the one measured here.

     The element still carries its digits, so the box measures itself from the
     same string the mask draws; `color: transparent` because the glass is what
     should be seen, not ink over it. The reading order keeps a real number in
     a visually hidden sibling — a masked pane with transparent text is a
     picture as far as a screen reader is concerned.

     The numeral still had to LEAVE the card: glass-lens.js refuses a lens to
     anything whose parent sits inside another glass host, and a backdrop-filter
     element is a backdrop root for its descendants, so a nested pane would
     refract the card's composite and never the bed. The slot holds the two as
     siblings and carries the drift, which is what keeps them registered. */
  .mslot { position: relative; }
  .mnum-masks { position: absolute; width: 0; height: 0; }
  /* OVER THE CARD, not across its corner. Straddling the edge put three
     quarters of the figure on the bed, which on this section is near-black: a
     glass glyph there has nothing behind it to bend and reads as absent. Its
     own gutter inside the card puts it over the curtains instead, which are
     the strongest light on the surface — and the card's rounded corner stops
     cutting it. */
  /* THE LIGHT BEHIND THE FIGURE. A lens adds no light of its own — it shows
     what is behind it — so a glass glyph over a quiet backdrop stays quiet
     however thick the glass is. This is a second copy of the same digits,
     unmasked and unfilled, carrying nothing but a text-shadow: the glow has to
     live OUTSIDE the mask, because the mask is the letterform and anything
     beyond it is cut.

     Two shadows, not one: a tight bright core that gives the strokes an edge
     to read against, and a wide soft halo that lifts the figure off the card.
     Both from the light pole the rest of the material is made of, so the
     numeral is lit by the same light as the pane it sits in. */
  .mpoint__glow,
  .mpoint__no {
    position: absolute;
    left: clamp(0.9rem, 2.4vw, 1.7rem);
    top: 50%;
    translate: 0 -50%;
    font-family: var(--sl-font-mono);
    font-weight: 200;
    font-size: var(--step-numeral);
    line-height: 0.8;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: transparent;
    /* ROOM FOR THE INK. A mask clips to its own rect and a backdrop-filter to
       the border box, so anything a glyph draws outside the element is cut —
       and line-height 0.8 makes the box SHORTER than the letter: measured,
       92.6px of ink in an 89.6px box, and the advance over the max-content
       width by another 4.5px. The result was a numeral trimmed top and right
       while the glow, which carries neither a mask nor a filter, spilled
       freely past it.

       On both copies, so the two boxes grow together and the figures stay
       registered. */
    padding: 0.09em 0.07em;
  }
  .mpoint__glow {
    z-index: 1;
    text-shadow:
      0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--sl-text-primary) 46%, transparent),
      0 0 30px color-mix(in srgb, var(--sl-text-primary) 26%, transparent);
  }
  .mpoint__no {
    z-index: 2;
    display: block;
    width: max-content;
    /* No radius: the mask is the shape now, and a rounded rect under a
       letterform mask only rounds corners nobody can see. */
    --slg-radius: 0;
    /* AND NO RIM. glass.css gives a pane its thickness with an inset shadow
       along the top of its BOX; masked to a letterform that line survives
       wherever a glyph meets the box's top edge, and it read as a white bar
       across the digits. A box rim is meaningless on a letter — what gives
       these their edge is the glow behind them — so both poles go to
       transparent, which is the kit's own way of standing the layer down
       (the side stop is a color-mix over --slg-rim-hi and follows it). */
    --slg-rim-hi: transparent;
    --slg-rim-lo: transparent;
  }

  .mpoint__verb {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-display);
    font-weight: 440;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 72, 'WONK' 1;
    font-size: var(--step-h3);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--sl-text-primary);
  }
  .mpoint__body p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: var(--sl-text-secondary);
  }
  .method__head .lede strong,
  .mpoint__body :global(strong) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--sl-text-primary);
  }
</style>
